s, I can bet for value, otherwise, I can re-evaluate. I have no made hand, so I don't want to build a huge pot. If I bet and get raised, I'm in a tough spot and will have to fold. Checking lets me realize my equity for free.\n\n**Evaluation: I have King high with two overcards (K, J) on a board of 10-5-6-6. The opponent has checked three times, indicating weakness or a draw. I have no made hand but decent showdown value against a random range.**\n**Reason: Checking is the optimal play. It allows me to see the river card for free, potentially improving to top pair. Betting risks a raise or calling station behavior when I have no made hand, and folding is illogical when I can check.**\n**Answer: 0**\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: You hold King-Jack offsuit on a board of 10-5-6-6. You currently have King-high with the board's pair of 6s. This is a marginal hand, but your two overcards (King and Jack) give you reasonable equity if you pair up on the river. The opponent has checked three times (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn), signaling a weak hand or a very slow play. Since the pot is small and you have no made hand yet, there is no strong incentive to build the pot.\n\nReason: Checking is the most prudent action. It allows you to see the River card for free, giving you a chance to hit a King or Jack to likely win the pot. Betting risks getting raised or called by a low pair, forcing you to invest more chips with only high-card value. Folding is unnecessary as checking costs nothing.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
